<span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Buying vs Renting</span>
<h1>Students: Ownership Paperwork Questions for Hanoi Motorbikes</h1>
<p>A motorbike is two things, metal that moves and paper that permits, and the student who verifies only the metal has inspected half the purchase. Ownership paperwork is where secondhand deals in Hanoi quietly succeed or loudly collapse, and it deserves the same patient walk-around the machine itself gets. This guide collects the questions a student should ask before money changes hands, holds them to one standard, current official verification instead of remembered folklore, and points at the neutral desk where the whole map can be sanity-checked by people with nothing to sell.</p>
<p>The machines, the briefing and the honest numbers come from Motorbike Rental - Nguyen Tu, 112 Nguyen Van Cu in Long Bien, Hanoi, open daily 09:00 to 21:00, where the desk briefs students on both sides of the paperwork question honestly, because the shop rents machines and never sells them. Begin at the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-for-sale/">motorbike for sale hub</a>, study the long-stay arrangements on the <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental</a> page, check the verified figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, review the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> for maintenance and safety, and keep <a href="/renting-a-motorbike-in-vietnam/">renting a motorbike in Vietnam</a> open for the legal layer.</p>
